    37	1. **The one-day slice** (2026-07-01, one draw, run cap $0.25): `zsh clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/run_budgeted_draw.sh <folder>
    38	   <ledger> 2026-07-01 2026-07-01 0.25` (a manifest with the configuration's, the items database's and the code's sha256, a log, a pid
    39	   file; the shared ledger made once with `budget_replay.py --init-ledger <ledger> --ceiling 5.00`). It completes, opens its own views,
    40	   judges them, and the readers above read its views, rungs, judgements and requests. A failure stops and goes to the council.
    41	2. **Three draws**, the same day, detached, one launcher each, NVDA, 2026-07-01..07-14, run cap $1.00 each, the same ledger. A draw that
    42	   does not complete is re-run once from empty if the ledger leaves room; otherwise, or on a second failure, it is left out, reported.
    43	3. **Opened questions:** every view each production draw created (none is seeded): question, rungs, due day.
    44	4. **Matching** to the 23 ruler questions under `clean-arch/unseeded-2026-11-10/MATCHING-v2.md`, with the brief and procedure the clean
    45	   readers used, written down before the readers start: two families blind (labels hidden, keys outside the repository), a third reader
    46	   on each disagreement. The clean arm's matching (`mapping-runs.json`) stands as made. For one ruler question, the candidate views
    47	   resolve as: any SAME confirmed: matched; none SAME and at least one unresolved: "unresolved"; every candidate NOT SAME, or no
    48	   candidate at all: "not opened in the ruler's shape".
    49	5. **Eligibility** (question, period and quantity the ruler's; due day the ruler's, D430) by two blind readers with a third on
    50	   disagreement, the same brief for both arms; the literal reading (the same due day) beside.
    51	6. **Outcomes, per ruler question, per draw, per arm, the first that applies:** (i) reader B's gold open or no gold view: "not
    52	   graded"; (ii) no candidate view confirmed SAME and at least one unresolved after the third reader (point 4's rule): "unresolved", excluded
    53	   from opening counts;
    54	   (iii) no view matched SAME: "not opened in the ruler's shape"; (iv) several views matched SAME: the one created first is judged, the
    55	   others listed (an unresolved candidate beside a confirmed one is listed and does not change the outcome); (v) eligibility unresolved:
    56	   "unresolved"; (vi) not eligible: "opened, not eligible", with the reason; (vii) no judgement on or before 2026-07-14: "no judgement";
    57	   (viii) a figure rung with no bound: "no comparison"; (ix) agrees or disagrees, by the frozen scorer's rule (`prod_records.outcome`
    58	   for production; the same rule for the clean arm's ladders, as Stage B counted them).
    59	7. **Each lost graded answer traced fact by fact** (outcomes iii and vi–ix-disagree). For each of reader B's decisive items for that
    60	   question: *extracted* (a kept evidence item of the run states it), *linked in time* (linked to the matched view before the last
    61	   judgement on or before the window end), *shown* (listed in a judge request for that view on or before that day:
    62	   `prod_records.shown_evidence`, and Stage B's request search for the clean arm). Two readers mark extracted and linked against the
    63	   item text; code reads shown. **Cause, the first that applies:** (a) opening (outcome iii or vi); (b) not judged (outcome vii): the
    64	   facts' marks are listed as observations, never as the cause of a judgement that does not exist, and never as a judgement error;
    65	   (c) some decisive item never extracted; (d) some extracted item never linked in time; (e) some linked item never shown; (f) every
    66	   decisive item shown and the view's rungs cannot express reader B's range (no rung band overlaps it, or a different measure);
    67	   (g) every item shown and the rungs could express it: judgement; (h) the readers cannot settle a fact: "ambiguous". A loss with
    68	   several causes takes the first in this order and lists the rest; each decisive item's three marks are reported.
    69	8. **Report:** per arm and draw, each graded question's outcome and cause; every other ruler question's outcome; the clean arm's
    70	   numbers read from its stored chain under the same rules. No pass line.
    71	
    72	## Spending: an enforced shared ceiling
    73	
    74	Every production run (the slice, three draws, at most one re-run) runs through `budget_replay.py`: production's own replay
    75	(`hivemind.replay.run`, the arguments `scripts/replay.py` passes, its seeded-ledger refusal imported from it) with one change, the
    76	transport the production code already accepts, `BudgetTransport`. Before each attempt it reserves that attempt's worst cost on one
    77	ledger shared by every run under a lock: a language-model request (2 × its JSON bytes + 4,096) input tokens and its whole output cap at
    78	`model.PRICES`; a Jev request the same input bound times Jev's three internal attempts, and every Jev call settles at that whole
    79	reservation (`jev.transport` may have sent unanswered attempts that were billed). A language-model attempt goes out once and is
    80	sent again (up to three, production's pauses) only when the service gave no answer (no HTTP status, 429 or 5xx). Every response
    81	settles at its usage, every failed attempt and every usage-less response at its reservation; a response above its reservation stops
    82	all later attempts. An attempt that would pass the ceiling is not sent: production records that item or judgement as failed, and the
    83	draw counts as incomplete. **Why the reservation bounds a call's cost.** Input: a token covers at least one byte of the text it encodes, so a request's input tokens
    84	cannot exceed its message bytes plus the few formatting tokens per message, which 4,096 covers; the reservation takes twice the whole
    85	JSON's bytes. Measured on all 1,609 stored `gpt-6-luna` calls of the seeded v116 runs, the largest ratio of input tokens to request bytes
    86	is 0.255 (a judging call, 10,694 tokens for 41,968 bytes), so the reservation is at least 7.8 times the largest observed input. Output:
    87	the provider stops a completion at `max_completion_tokens`, and no stored call's output exceeded it (0 of 1,609). The $5.00 holds under
    88	these two bounds; if a response ever exceeds its reservation, the overrun flag refuses every later attempt and the excess is reported.
    89	**Ceiling: $5.00 for all production runs** (expected about $0.5–0.7 a draw; the reservations of the calls in
    90	flight need about $0.5 of room), with the run caps beside (`--cap` 0.25 for the slice, 1.00 for each draw). Total under D439 at most
    91	$4.77 + $5.00 = $9.77 of $40.
    92	
    93	Checks (`check_budget.py`, fake services, no network): 16 threads × 20 calls under a ceiling of 5.5 reservations: spent plus reserved
    94	never passed the ceiling, some calls refused, nothing left reserved; Jev's one-argument call reserves three attempts and settles at that
    95	whole reservation, and after one such call a second that would pass a ceiling of 1.5 reservations is refused (mutant: settle Jev at its
    96	reported usage, caught); two network failures then an answer: three attempts, the failures at their reservation; HTTP 400 sent once; an overrun refuses
    97	later attempts; a request without an output cap refused; a usage-less response settled at its reservation: 10 of 10, three runs after the last change. Mutants:
    98	no ceiling check (caught), every status retried (caught), failed attempts settled at zero (caught). The first control run caught a real
    99	defect: ledger reads outside the lock could see a half-written file, crash a thread and leave its reservation held; reads are now
   100	locked and writes atomic. (The numeric bound of version 2, $12.25, is replaced: it was an estimate.)
